WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.680
The tallest measured coast redwoods stand more than 100 m high.

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.440
Every leaf at the top gets water from roots in the soil.

3
00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:13.860
A tree has no heart and no pump.

4
00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:15.820
So what lifts the water?

5
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.720
The answer is the dry air around the leaves.

6
00:00:19.733 --> 00:00:22.493
Water potential predicts which way water moves.

7
00:00:23.513 --> 00:00:26.493
Pure water at atmospheric pressure is defined as zero.

8
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:30.313
Water always moves toward lower total water potential.

9
00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:32.413
Take illustrative values.

10
00:00:33.493 --> 00:00:34.933
Soil is minus 0.3.

11
00:00:36.033 --> 00:00:37.453
Root is minus 0.6.

12
00:00:38.493 --> 00:00:39.973
Leaf is minus 1.5.

13
00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:44.513
Air at 50% % relative humidity is about minus 94.

14
00:00:45.513 --> 00:00:46.873
Every step runs downhill.

15
00:00:47.393 --> 00:00:49.373
The biggest drop is from leaf to air.

16
00:00:50.367 --> 00:00:55.147
Inside a leaf, water evaporates from the wet walls of the inner photosynthetic

17
00:00:55.147 --> 00:01:00.047
cells. The water left in the walls is held more tightly, and its pressure falls.

18
00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:02.567
Water moves in from the xylem.

19
00:01:03.407 --> 00:01:08.047
Xylem cells that conduct are dead and hollow, a continuous pathway from root to

20
00:01:08.047 --> 00:01:13.687
leaf. Cohesion, the attraction between water molecules, lets a thin column in a

21
00:01:13.687 --> 00:01:15.347
narrow tube be pulled like a rope.

22
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:18.170
Gravity sets a floor on the pull.

23
00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:24.330
Water potential rises by about 0.01 MPa for each metre of height.

24
00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:32.570
A leaf 30 m up needs 0.30 MPa of extra tension just to hold a still column, before

25
00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:39.370
any flow. At the top of a 110 m redwood, gravity alone adds over 1 MPa.

26
00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:42.203
Could the roots push water up instead?

27
00:01:43.043 --> 00:01:45.503
In woody plants and vines, root pressure is small.

28
00:01:46.263 --> 00:01:53.293
The highest value in a survey of 109 tropical vines and woody species was about 0.15

29
00:01:53.293 --> 00:01:58.723
MPa. That is enough in principle to raise water about 15 m.

30
00:01:59.683 --> 00:02:02.543
It is weak or absent when transpiration is fastest.

31
00:02:03.443 --> 00:02:06.763
Cut the xylem of a transpiring tree, and air is drawn in.

32
00:02:06.763 --> 00:02:08.683
Sap does not spurt out.

33
00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:13.627
A vertebrate heart pushes blood round a closed loop at positive pressure.

34
00:02:14.487 --> 00:02:21.247
A typical peak arterial pressure in a resting adult human is about 0.016 MPa.

35
00:02:22.047 --> 00:02:26.987
In a transpiring plant, xylem water moves mainly one way, root to leaf, under

36
00:02:26.987 --> 00:02:27.987
negative pressure.

37
00:02:28.787 --> 00:02:30.067
Evaporation powers it.

38
00:02:30.987 --> 00:02:32.507
Xylem water is pulled, not pushed.
